      "source_example_id": "e-7195",
      "knowledge_point": "Swapping the Order of Integration on a Partitioned Region",
      "kp_id": "e-7195"
    },
    "q-82353": {
      "dom_id": "step-q82353",
      "name": "Question 11",
      "problem": "${\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{x/2}^{1/2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x=$",
      "worked_solution": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/foundations/14154008/assets/b6b672e4776973f14b8ecff1fc35bcdd50c4229ade12ec74dc09c5327223c25f.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the region of integration. This is a type I region, and its type I representation is\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,\\frac{x}{2}\\le y\\le \\frac{1}{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nNotice that the left boundary of the region, written in the form $y=y(x)$ is\n\n$$\nx=0,\n$$\n\nand the right boundary of the region, written in the form $y=y(x),$ is\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{x}{2}\\,⟹\\,x=2y.\n$$\n\nTo change the order of integration, we write $D$ as type II region. Its type II representation is\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le \\frac{1}{2},\\,0\\le x\\le 2y\\},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/foundations/14154008/assets/09f6f71eaf66fe10e73b80ae59a30ddd04478de71dbe37c749674ac9f10d5bd4.png)\n\nTherefore, swapping the order of integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{x/2}^{1/2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1/2}{\\int }_{0}^{2y}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1/2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({y}^{2})[{\\int }_{0}^{2y}\\text{d}x]\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1/2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({y}^{2})[x]_{0}^{2y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1/2}2y\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡({y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =[-\\operatorname{cos}⁡({y}^{2})]_{0}^{1/2} \\\\  & =1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{1}{4}).\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
