      "name": "Question 13",
      "problem": "${\\int }_{0}^{4}{\\int }_{\\sqrt{x}}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡({y}^{3}+\\pi )\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x=$",
      "worked_solution": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/foundations/14154008/assets/19d2d5698ad6b22c003b44d1a023c56ae7779a6d26345732bace3723062f6e0d.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the region of integration. This is a type I region, and its type I representation is\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 4,\\,\\sqrt{x}\\le y\\le 2\\}.\n$$\n\nNotice that the left boundary of the region, written in the form $y=y(x)$ is\n\n$$\nx=0,\n$$\n\nand the right boundary of the region, written in the form $y=y(x),$ is\n\n$$\ny=\\sqrt{x}\\,⟹\\,x={y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTo change the order of integration, we write $D$ as type II region. Its type II representation is\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 2,\\,0\\le x\\le {y}^{2}\\},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/foundations/14154008/assets/81596115241d43f3935ba9e055a3c748c1c1ae283aa2a6a372492ee8c68170cd.png)\n\nTherefore, swapping the order of integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{4}{\\int }_{\\sqrt{x}}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡({y}^{3}+\\pi )\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{{y}^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡({y}^{3}+\\pi )\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡({y}^{3}+\\pi )[{\\int }_{0}^{{y}^{2}}\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡({y}^{3}+\\pi )[x]_{0}^{{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{y}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡({y}^{3}+\\pi )\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡({y}^{3}+\\pi )}{3}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(8+\\pi )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi ) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(8+\\pi )}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
